    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0130" n="18" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg18"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conference 180.03101/11</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Secretary’s Notes of a Conversation, held in M.
                        Pichon’s Room at the Quai d’Orsay, Paris, on Tuesday, January 21, 1919, at
                        10:30 a.m.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg></opener>
                <p rendition="#right">B.C.–6</p>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Present</hi>.—<hi rendition="#italic">America,
                            United States of:</hi> President Wilson, Mr. R. Lansing, Mr. A. H.
                        Frazier, Col. U. S. Grant, Mr. L. Harrison.—<hi rendition="#italic">British
                            Empire:</hi> The Rt. Hon. D. Lloyd George, Prime Minister, The Rt. Hon.
                        A. J. Balfour, Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, Lieut.-Col. Sir M. P.
                        A. Hankey, K.C.B., Secretary, War Cabinet, Mr. H. Norman, Capt. E.
                            Abraham.—<hi rendition="#italic">France:</hi> M. Clemenceau, President
                        of the Council and Minister for War, M. Pichon, Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs, M. Dutasta, M. Berthelot, Capt. A. Portier.—<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Italy:</hi> Signor Orlando, President of the Council, His Excellency
                        Baron Sonnino, <pb facs="0131" n="19" xml:id="pg19"/> Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs, Count Aldrovandi, Major A. Jones.—<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Japan:</hi> Baron Makino, His Excellency M. Matsui, M. Saburi.—<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Interpreter:</hi> Professor P. J. Mantoux</item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p rendition="#center">13. Bolshevik overtures</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">President Wilson</hi> read to the meeting the
                    telegraphic report annexed (Annex “A”) from Mr. Butler [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Buckler</hi>], who had interviewed Litvinoff in Stockholm.<anchor n="22"
                        xml:id="d16-f1"/></p>
                <p>(It was agreed that this letter should also be translated and discussed at the
                    afternoon meeting.)</p>
                <p>14. …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Villa Majestic</hi>, <lb/>
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1919-01-21"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">January 21, 1919</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d16-f1" n="22" xml:id="d16-f1-content">
                <p>The report (not here printed) consisted of Mr. Buckler’s two telegrams
                    transmitted by the Chargé in Denmark, nos. 116 and 118, Jan. 18 and Jan. 19, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg15">pp. 15</ref>, <ref
                        target="#pg17">17</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
